# A card in full colour: HyperMarkDown for VS Code 0.7.0 { .hql-post-title }

<p class="hql-post-meta">25 November 2026 · Yauhen Yakimovich</p>

!!! abstract "TL;DR"

    - **HQL has colours.** A `.hql` file is highlighted, and so is the body
      of an `hql`, `hql#eval` or `hql#query` fence in a card.
    - **A cell in any language is coloured as that language.**
      `python#eval id=plot` is Python now, not one grey block.
    - **A fence may name any of 770 languages.** The list is the one GitHub
      uses, from GitHub Linguist 9.7.0.
    - **A language server comes with the extension.** A link that names no
      card is coloured as an error while you type. Hover and **Go to
      Definition** work on links.
    - **This post is a card.** Its source is a `.hmd` file, and the last
      section shows it.

I write almost everything in cards now. A card is a
[HyperMarkDown](https://hypermarkdown.org/) file: Markdown, plus links that
name other cards, plus code that can run. The design notes of HQL are cards.
So are the issues, the book, and this blog. I read and edit all of it in
VS Code.

Until this week, that editor showed a lot of grey.

The prose was fine. Headings, links and emphasis had their colours. The code
did not. An HQL query in a card was a flat block of one colour. A Python cell
was the same from the moment it got a mode. A card about a data pipeline
could hold four languages, and the editor drew all four alike.

Version 0.7.0 of the extension came out today, and it fixes that. Here is
what changed, in the order I noticed it.

## HQL has colours

This is a script from the
[[/blog/hql-0-8-0|HQL 0.8.0 post]]. It counts the commits of each author in
a repository:

```hql
#!/usr/bin/env hql
from io.process import *

source(command("git", ["log", "--format=%an"]), Lines)
| group(by = name => name)
| sort(by = g => g.key)
| map(g => {author: g.key, commits: g.items | size})
```

On this site it has always had colours. In the editor it had none, because
VS Code had no grammar for HQL. The extension now carries one.

It applies in two places. A `.hql` file is highlighted as HQL. In a card,
the body of an `hql` fence is highlighted too, and so is the body of an
`hql#eval` or an `hql#query` cell.

Two smaller things come with the grammar. **Toggle Line Comment** inside a
cell writes an HQL comment, not a Markdown one. Bracket matching inside a
cell follows HQL's brackets.

## A cell keeps the colours of its language

A **cell** is a fence with a mode after the language name. The mode is
written after a `#`, and attributes may follow it:

````markdown
```python#eval id=plot
import matplotlib.pyplot as plt

plt.plot([1, 2, 3], [2, 4, 8])
plt.savefig("growth.png")
```
````

The Markdown grammar of VS Code reads a language name up to the end of the
word. It does not read `python#eval`, so it gave up on the whole fence and
drew the body in one colour. The plain fence was Python. The cell was grey.

In 0.7.0 the body of this cell is coloured as Python. The same holds for a
cell in any other language. The parts of the fence line have their own
colours as well: the mode `eval`, the attribute `id=plot`, and the
`hql:output` markers that hold a cell's saved output.

## 770 languages

I wanted to know how many language names a fence accepts now, so I counted
the rows of the table the extension ships. There are **770**.

The table comes from
[GitHub Linguist](https://github.com/github-linguist/linguist) 9.7.0.
Linguist is the library GitHub uses to detect the language of a file and to
colour it. Its list has 770 languages with a grammar, and the extension
takes all of them.

A fence can name a language in three ways:

- by its **name**: `kotlin`, `toml`, `rust`
- by one of its **aliases**. The list has 416 of them, such as `c#`
- by a **file extension** that only one language uses, such as `kt`

So the fences you already write on GitHub work in a card without changes:

```kotlin
data class Book(val title: String, val status: String)

val todo = shelf.filter { it.status == "todo" }
```

```toml
location_path = "."
```

The list goes well past the usual names. COBOL and Fortran are in it. So are
Zig, D2, Brainfuck and LOLCODE.

!!! note "The extension brings the names, not 770 grammars"

    The body of a fence is coloured when VS Code has a grammar for that
    language. VS Code ships grammars for the common languages, and an
    installed extension adds its own. For a language with no grammar
    installed, the fence line is coloured and the body is not. Install the
    extension for that language and the body gets its colours.

## Links are checked while you type

Colour is the part you see first. The second part of 0.7.0 is about links.

A card links to another card by name: `[[reading-list]]`. Before 0.7.0 a
link looked the same whether its target existed or not, until the linter
ran. Now the extension starts `hmd-lsp`, the HyperMarkDown language server,
for every `.hmd` file. With it:

- a link that names no card is coloured as an error. So is a link that
  names several cards, and one that leaves the vault
- **hover** on a link says which card it resolves to
- **Go to Definition** opens that card

There is nothing to install. The build for each platform carries
`hmd-lsp` 0.1.0.

```d2
direction: down

card: "A card (.hmd)"
grammar: "Grammars\nMarkdown, HQL, 770 fence names"
server: "hmd-lsp\nthe language server"
colours: "Coloured code"
links: "Checked links\nerror colour, hover, Go to Definition"

card -> grammar
card -> server
grammar -> colours
server -> links
```

The two halves are separate. Without the server, the extension works as
before and the colours stay. The server never starts in an untrusted
workspace, and `hyperMarkdown.languageServer.enabled` turns it off.

The server is HyperMarkDown's, and it reads links. HQL's part in this
release is the grammar; HQL has no language server of its own. Completion
and rename of links are not written yet.

## This post is a card

The page you are reading is one `.hmd` file,
[`doc/public/blog/hmd-vscode-0-7-0.hmd`](https://github.com/ewiger/hql/blob/main/doc/public/blog/hmd-vscode-0-7-0.hmd)
in the HQL repository. The site builds it like any other card. Its first
lines are these:

````markdown
---
description: HyperMarkDown for VS Code 0.7.0 colours HQL, colours a cell …
---
# A card in full colour: HyperMarkDown for VS Code 0.7.0 { .hql-post-title }

…

This is a script from the
[[/blog/hql-0-8-0|HQL 0.8.0 post]]. It counts the commits of each author in
a repository:

```hql
#!/usr/bin/env hql
from io.process import *
```
````

Everything this post describes can be seen on the post itself. Open the file
in VS Code with 0.7.0 installed:

- the HQL script near the top is coloured as HQL
- the Kotlin and TOML fences are coloured as Kotlin and TOML
- the link to the HQL 0.8.0 post resolves, and hover says where it goes.
  Change one letter of its name and it turns into an error
- the diagram above is a `d2` fence in the text, and the preview draws it

That is the reason I like the format. The post about the tool is a file the
tool reads.

## Install

```text
ext install hypermarkdown.hmd
```

- **VS Code**: search *HyperMarkDown* in the Extensions view, or open
  [the marketplace listing](https://marketplace.visualstudio.com/items?itemName=hypermarkdown.hmd).
- **Cursor, Windsurf, VSCodium**:
  [Open VSX](https://open-vsx.org/extension/hypermarkdown/hmd).

It needs VS Code 1.90 or newer. Then open any `.hmd` file. The
[extension's page](https://hypermarkdown.org/tools/vscode/) has the rest,
and the
[changelog](https://github.com/ewiger/hypermarkdown/blob/main/tools/hmd-vsc-ext/CHANGELOG.md)
lists every change in 0.7.0.

Questions and bug reports are welcome on
[r/hqlang](https://www.reddit.com/r/hqlang/).
